---
scope: src/cli
alignment: unreviewed
description: "The package's terminal commands, each routed to the one command that does that job: typed by a human outside any agent session, except one called by Claude Code hooks."
intent: >
  This module is the package's terminal front door, mostly for a human at a
  plain terminal outside any agent session. It routes the subcommand to the
  one command that handles it, such as installing, syncing or viewing
  pipeline state, and loads only that command, so asking for one never pays
  for the server or the others. Each command a human types does one job and
  tells them what it did, or why it could not, leaving the project
  untouched when it fails. One command is called by Claude Code hooks
  instead. Adding a command never changes the others.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A person can run any human-typed command from a plain terminal with no agent session, agent harness or prior agent setup in place.
    - Running one command loads only that command. It never starts the MCP server or pays the startup cost of any other command.
    - Each command a human types does one job and ends by telling the human, in a few clear lines, what it did.
    - A human-typed command that cannot do its job says why in plain language and exits with a failure status, leaving every file it would have written untouched.
    - Adding a new command leaves every existing command's behavior, output and exit status unchanged.
    - The one command called by Claude Code hooks instead of a person answers the hook, not a human, and never fails, prompts or slows the agent's work.
  undesired:
    - A human-typed command that only works inside an agent session, or prints agent-shaped output such as tool-result payloads or instructions meant for a model instead of short human-readable lines.
    - Running one command starts the MCP server or loads other commands' work, so a quick command becomes slow or fails because of an unrelated one.
    - A failed command that leaves partial writes behind, or reports success and exits zero when it did not finish its job.
    - A human-typed command that finishes silently, or buries what it did under verbose logs, so the human cannot tell what changed.
    - A command whose behavior, output or exit status shifts because another command was added or changed.
    - The hook-called command prompting, printing for a person, failing or taking long enough to slow the agent session that called it.
---
